"""
Obstacle challenge, merged:
  - Wall following: ported from the open-challenge script (single-side
    "lowest wall point" PD tracker + dynamic target point per turn count),
    rescaled onto THIS robot's servo range (default_servo / max_turn_degree
    / STEER_SIGN below) instead of the open challenge's raw values.
  - Polygon detection + red border: now ported EXACTLY like the reference
    repo (ImageDrawingUtils.find_contour + draw_polygon): find the contour
    that contains the point directly in front of the robot, approxPolyDP
    with a tight epsilon, fillPoly to get the mask, bitwise_and against
    the clean binary image. No dilation margin, no custom "biggest
    contour" heuristic -- same steps, same order they use.
  - Color detection: switched from Lab to HSV throughout (calculate_color_mask),
    as plain module-level constants + a function -- no color-utility class.
  - Pillar detection + avoidance: ported from the reference repo's
    filter_pillars()/closest-distance-to-robot loop -- always finds the
    single red-or-green blob whose center is closest to the robot's
    bottom-center point, with no ROI restriction and no minimum-y gate.
    It just always picks whichever blob (above the area threshold) is
    nearest, full stop.
  - Crash avoidance (SIMPLIFIED, corner-anticipating): 3 inner dots + 1
    outer dot are checked every frame. If any of the 3 inner dots sees
    black, hard-steer AWAY from that inner wall until all 3 are clear
    again. If the outer dot sees black, hard-steer INTO the turn
    direction until it's clear again. When wall-follow's own corner
    detector (`is_corner`, from a sudden jump in its PD error) fires,
    both checks temporarily add an extra, further-out detection point so
    a real corner trips the crash override a frame or two sooner. This
    directly overrides wall-follow and pillar avoidance while active --
    no passive "warning" state that lets the robot keep drifting into
    the wall.
  - Turning: corner-marker detection is a pure lap counter now; it does
    not touch steering. Cornering is handled entirely by wall-follow's
    continuous PD math plus the crash/pillar overrides above.
  - Lap counting (2026-07-30, hysteresis update): the corner marker is
    direction-aware -- LEFT only ever watches the BLUE line, RIGHT only
    ever watches the ORANGE line, masked against the current track
    polygon (not a fixed ROI box). The entry/exit debounce now uses TWO
    thresholds with a dead-band between them (LINE_AREA_ENTER_THRESHOLD
    higher, LINE_AREA_EXIT_THRESHOLD lower) instead of one shared
    threshold, plus longer TURN_MIN_DURATION/EXIT_CONFIRM_FRAMES/
    TURN_COOLDOWN. This fixes an earlier bug where a single physical
    corner marker got counted TWICE: with one threshold and short
    timers, the marker mask could dip below threshold for a few frames
    mid-turn (camera angle change / partial occlusion while steering
    through the corner) -- long enough to be read as "line gone,
    quarter finished" -- and then reappear later in that SAME corner and
    get counted again as a brand-new entry, so 12 counted quarters could
    happen after only ~6 real corners. The dead-band means transient
    dips that don't fully clear the (lower) exit threshold can't flip
    the state, and the longer minimum/cooldown durations mean the
    detector can't finish and restart within a single real corner
    traversal. See the marker-detection block in run().
  - Exit-parking direction calibration (2026-07-30): before ANY other
    logic runs (lap counting, wall-follow, obstacle avoidance), the
    robot drives straight for a few frames while checking two fixed
    points against the floor mask -- one near the left edge of the
    frame, one near the right edge. Whichever side sees the wall first
    tells us which wall the robot is closest to as it leaves the bay,
    so the robot should follow the OPPOSITE wall for the rest of the
    run (left point sees wall -> follow the right wall; right point
    sees wall -> follow the left wall). Direction is printed once,
    locked, and this check never runs again -- everything else proceeds
    exactly as before, just using the locked `direction`. See
    detect_exit_parking_direction() and the calibration gate in run().
  - Exit-turn maneuver (2026-07-30): immediately after direction is
    locked, a one-time three-point-turn state machine runs before the
    main wall-follow loop takes over:
        1. "turning"      -- steer hard INTO the locked direction and
                              drive forward, watching a single dot near
                              the TOP of the frame. As soon as that dot
                              sees black (the front wall getting close),
                              this phase ends.
        2. "counter_turn"  -- steer hard the OPPOSITE way for a fixed
                              duration (EXIT_COUNTER_TURN_DURATION),
                              still driving forward, to swing the back
                              end around.
        3. "backing_up"    -- reverse the motor with the wheels
                              straight for a fixed duration
                              (EXIT_BACKUP_DURATION) to back out of the
                              corner the turn put it in.
        4. "done"          -- motor back to forward drive, and the main
                              loop (lap counting, wall-follow, obstacle
                              avoidance) takes over from here on, exactly
                              as before.
    See check_top_wall(), the EXIT_* constants below, and the maneuver
    state machine in run().

HSV ranges below (BLUE_LOWER/UPPER, RED_LOWER_1/2, etc.) are tuned for the
actual track/lighting. GRAY_THRESHOLD, kp/kd, and the crash-point offsets
may still need further tuning once you see it running.

=========================================================================
SERVO-INVERSION FIX (2026-07-29)
=========================================================================
The servo horn/linkage was remounted upside down, so a raw PWM angle that
used to steer the wheels RIGHT now physically steers them LEFT, and vice
versa. Rather than touch any of the steering math (PD control, crash
avoidance targets, pillar avoidance, slew-rate limiting all still reason
in the ORIGINAL convention: MIN_ANGLE steers right, MAX_ANGLE steers
left), the fix mirrors the final angle around `default_servo` at the one
and only point it's handed to the servo queue -- see `to_physical_angle()`
and its use in `run()`. Since MIN_ANGLE = default_servo - max_turn_degree
and MAX_ANGLE = default_servo + max_turn_degree, mirroring around the
center exactly swaps MIN_ANGLE <-> MAX_ANGLE, so the whole valid range
maps onto itself -- no extra clamping needed, and every other part of the
code keeps working in the same coordinate system it always has.

If the servo ever gets remounted right-side up again, just flip
STEER_SIGN back to 1 and this correction switches off automatically.
"""

import cv2
import math
